WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.979
Artificial intelligence can be organized
around agents.

2
00:00:03.979 --> 00:00:10.140
An agent receives percepts through sensors and
affects an environment through actuators.

3
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:16.278
A thermostat senses temperature and controls
heating; a game agent observes a board and

4
00:00:16.278 --> 00:00:17.155
selects moves.

5
00:00:17.155 --> 00:00:22.629
Calling an action intelligent requires a
performance objective and information

6
00:00:22.629 --> 00:00:23.176
assumptions.

7
00:00:23.176 --> 00:00:29.711
A rational agent selects the action expected
to perform best given its percept history and

8
00:00:29.711 --> 00:00:34.068
knowledge, not one guaranteed to succeed in
every uncertain world.

9
00:00:34.068 --> 00:00:40.201
The environment may be fully or partially
observable, deterministic or stochastic,

10
00:00:40.201 --> 00:00:41.874
static or changing.

11
00:00:41.874 --> 00:00:46.829
These properties determine which
representations and algorithms are

12
00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:47.448
appropriate.

13
00:00:47.448 --> 00:00:52.526
Begin by specifying the task environment
rather than choosing an AI technique because

14
00:00:52.526 --> 00:00:53.698
it is fashionable.

15
00:00:53.834 --> 00:01:00.183
A reflex agent maps the current situation to
an action, such as braking when an obstacle is

16
00:01:00.183 --> 00:01:00.556
close.

17
00:01:00.556 --> 00:01:05.710
Reflexes can be fast and effective when local
rules cover the environment.

18
00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:11.715
A planning agent represents possible future
states and evaluates action sequences before

19
00:01:11.715 --> 00:01:12.215
acting.

20
00:01:12.215 --> 00:01:17.459
Planning becomes valuable when an action's
benefit depends on later consequences or when

21
00:01:17.459 --> 00:01:18.669
several routes compete.

22
00:01:18.669 --> 00:01:25.792
It also has a cost: the state space can grow
rapidly, the model may be wrong, and the world

23
00:01:25.792 --> 00:01:27.291
may change during computation.

24
00:01:27.291 --> 00:01:33.261
Many real systems combine layers—fast safety
reflexes around a slower planner.

25
00:01:33.261 --> 00:01:39.893
The design question is not whether planning is
always smarter, but which consequences must be

26
00:01:39.893 --> 00:01:42.546
represented to meet the performance objective.

27
00:01:42.674 --> 00:01:45.583
Search requires a precise formulation.

28
00:01:45.583 --> 00:01:49.894
A state contains the information needed to
choose future actions.

29
00:01:49.894 --> 00:01:52.624
Actions specify available choices.

30
00:01:52.624 --> 00:01:55.442
The transition model predicts the next state.

31
00:01:55.442 --> 00:01:58.440
Action cost measures the value spent.

32
00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:04.728
The start state identifies the initial
condition, and a goal test recognizes success.

33
00:02:04.728 --> 00:02:11.146
In route finding, a state may be a location,
actions may follow roads, and cost may

34
00:02:11.146 --> 00:02:12.751
represent distance or time.

35
00:02:12.751 --> 00:02:18.789
Include too little state and the model cannot
predict consequences; include irrelevant

36
00:02:18.789 --> 00:02:22.815
detail and the search space becomes needlessly
large.

37
00:02:22.815 --> 00:02:27.904
The formulation determines what solutions mean
before any algorithm explores them.

38
00:02:27.904 --> 00:02:33.791
A correct algorithm on the wrong state
representation solves the wrong problem

39
00:02:33.791 --> 00:02:34.282
efficiently.

40
00:02:34.418 --> 00:02:39.368
The state space is a graph of possible
configurations and transitions.

41
00:02:39.368 --> 00:02:43.041
A search algorithm builds a tree of paths from
the start.

42
00:02:43.041 --> 00:02:47.607
The same state may appear multiple times
because different paths reach it.

43
00:02:47.607 --> 00:02:53.066
Tree search can therefore revisit cycles
forever or repeat expensive work.

44
00:02:53.066 --> 00:02:58.828
Graph search maintains an explored set or
best-known cost information to control

45
00:02:58.828 --> 00:02:59.788
repeated expansion.

46
00:02:59.788 --> 00:03:03.181
But duplicate handling must match the
algorithm.

47
00:03:03.181 --> 00:03:08.494
In cost-based search, reaching a known state
through a cheaper path may require updating

48
00:03:08.494 --> 00:03:09.253
the frontier.

49
00:03:09.253 --> 00:03:16.133
A search node often stores a state, parent,
action, path cost, and depth so the final

50
00:03:16.133 --> 00:03:18.283
action sequence can be reconstructed.

51
00:03:18.283 --> 00:03:22.773
State equality and hashing are part of the
algorithm's correctness.

52
00:03:22.898 --> 00:03:28.473
Breadth-first search uses a first-in,
first-out frontier and explores by depth.

53
00:03:28.473 --> 00:03:33.192
With equal action costs, the first goal found
has the fewest actions.

54
00:03:33.192 --> 00:03:36.113
Its frontier can require large memory.

55
00:03:36.113 --> 00:03:41.151
Depth-first search uses a stack and pursues
one branch before backtracking.

56
00:03:41.151 --> 00:03:47.484
It can use less frontier memory but may spend
a long time down an unhelpful branch and does

57
00:03:47.484 --> 00:03:49.595
not generally find the shortest solution.

58
00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:54.200
Graph search and finite-state assumptions
affect completeness.

59
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.472
Neither strategy understands action cost
beyond depth.

60
00:03:58.472 --> 00:04:03.779
Choose according to the guarantee the problem
needs, not a slogan that one is faster.

61
00:04:03.779 --> 00:04:09.930
A small example with unequal edge costs
exposes why fewest actions and least total

62
00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.687
cost are different objectives.

63
00:04:11.834 --> 00:04:16.426
Uniform-cost search orders the frontier by
path cost g of n.

64
00:04:16.426 --> 00:04:21.796
It may explore a deeper path before a shallow
one when the deeper path is cheaper.

65
00:04:21.796 --> 00:04:27.268
With nonnegative costs and appropriate
graph-search handling, the first removed goal

66
00:04:27.268 --> 00:04:28.263
is optimal.

67
00:04:28.263 --> 00:04:33.582
The algorithm needs a priority queue and must
track the best cost found for states.

68
00:04:33.582 --> 00:04:39.602
A cheaper path to an existing frontier state
should replace or supersede the expensive one.

69
00:04:39.602 --> 00:04:44.130
Uniform-cost search uses no estimate of
remaining distance, so it can expand many

70
00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:48.658
states in directions that are cheap so far but
far from a goal.

71
00:04:48.658 --> 00:04:54.141
It provides a baseline for understanding how
informed search adds guidance without giving

72
00:04:54.141 --> 00:04:55.406
up cost reasoning.

73
00:04:55.538 --> 00:05:01.342
A heuristic h of n uses problem knowledge to
estimate remaining cost.

74
00:05:01.342 --> 00:05:07.087
Straight-line distance can guide geographic
routing because it is inexpensive and related

75
00:05:07.087 --> 00:05:08.523
to travel distance.

76
00:05:08.523 --> 00:05:13.735
Greedy best-first search chooses the state
with smallest h, focusing on apparent

77
00:05:13.735 --> 00:05:16.776
closeness while ignoring the cost already
paid.

78
00:05:16.776 --> 00:05:21.865
It can be fast but is not generally optimal or
complete in all settings.

79
00:05:21.865 --> 00:05:26.112
A heuristic should be cheaper to compute than
the search work it saves.

80
00:05:26.112 --> 00:05:30.870
Zero is always an uninformative baseline for
nonnegative costs.

81
00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:36.224
Better heuristics distinguish promising states
while respecting the guarantees required by

82
00:05:36.224 --> 00:05:37.197
the algorithm.

83
00:05:37.197 --> 00:05:43.204
Heuristic design is representation design: it
encodes a simplified view of the problem.

84
00:05:43.346 --> 00:05:50.872
A star balances the cost accumulated so far,
g, with the heuristic estimate to the goal, h.

85
00:05:50.872 --> 00:05:55.324
If h is zero, A star behaves like uniform-cost
search.

86
00:05:55.324 --> 00:05:59.558
If g is ignored, behavior resembles greedy
search.

87
00:05:59.558 --> 00:06:03.449
The sum estimates total solution cost through
a frontier node.

88
00:06:03.449 --> 00:06:09.201
An admissible heuristic never overestimates
the true remaining optimal cost.

89
00:06:09.201 --> 00:06:15.323
Consistency requires that the heuristic at one
state be no greater than one-step cost plus

90
00:06:15.323 --> 00:06:17.364
the heuristic at the successor.

91
00:06:17.364 --> 00:06:23.104
Under standard conditions, these properties
support optimality and efficient graph-search

92
00:06:23.104 --> 00:06:23.678
handling.

93
00:06:23.678 --> 00:06:30.077
A star's memory use can still be substantial,
and a highly accurate heuristic may itself be

94
00:06:30.077 --> 00:06:30.477
expensive.

95
00:06:30.477 --> 00:06:34.941
Guarantees depend on the exact assumptions and
implementation.

96
00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:38.649
A returned path is only one dimension of
quality.

97
00:06:38.649 --> 00:06:44.602
Completeness asks whether an algorithm will
find a solution when one exists under stated

98
00:06:44.602 --> 00:06:45.027
conditions.

99
00:06:45.027 --> 00:06:49.823
Optimality asks whether the solution minimizes
the defined cost.

100
00:06:49.823 --> 00:06:55.563
Time and space can be estimated by states
expanded and stored, often in terms of

101
00:06:55.563 --> 00:06:57.476
branching factor and solution depth.

102
00:06:57.476 --> 00:07:03.772
Practical evaluation also measures frontier
size, duplicate handling, and heuristic

103
00:07:03.772 --> 00:07:04.402
computation.

104
00:07:04.402 --> 00:07:10.760
Most importantly, a mathematically optimal
path in an inaccurate model may be poor in the

105
00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:11.608
real environment.

106
00:07:11.608 --> 00:07:16.608
Costs, transitions, and goals must represent
what users actually value.

107
00:07:16.608 --> 00:07:22.192
Search evaluation joins algorithmic guarantees
with model validation rather than treating

108
00:07:22.192 --> 00:07:24.223
them as separate worlds.

109
00:07:24.362 --> 00:07:27.934
A complete search design answers nine
questions.

110
00:07:27.934 --> 00:07:31.952
What performance objective defines rational
behavior?

111
00:07:31.952 --> 00:07:34.490
What information does each state contain?

112
00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:37.283
Which actions and transitions are possible?

113
00:07:37.283 --> 00:07:39.222
What does path cost represent?

114
00:07:39.222 --> 00:07:41.531
What are the start and goal conditions?

115
00:07:41.531 --> 00:07:44.898
Which frontier rule matches the required
guarantee?

116
00:07:44.898 --> 00:07:48.342
How are repeated states and cheaper paths
handled?

117
00:07:48.342 --> 00:07:53.317
If a heuristic is used, what does it estimate
and what properties can be justified?

118
00:07:53.317 --> 00:07:59.505
Finally, how do time, memory, solution
quality, and model error behave on

119
00:07:59.505 --> 00:08:00.536
representative cases?

120
00:08:00.536 --> 00:08:05.026
Artificial intelligence is not produced by
naming an algorithm.

121
00:08:05.026 --> 00:08:10.473
It comes from connecting a clear environment
model to a decision process whose behavior can

122
00:08:10.473 --> 00:08:11.926
be explained and tested.
